WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:07.472
Thank you, everyone, for coming.

00:00:07.472 --> 00:00:09.430
We're really excited to
be speaking here today.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:10.570
My name is Mike Stackhouse.

00:00:10.570 --> 00:00:12.670
I'm the chief innovation
officer of Atorud Research.

00:00:12.670 --> 00:00:14.545
And with me, I have
Jessica Higgins, director

00:00:14.545 --> 00:00:16.022
of analytics engineering.

00:00:16.022 --> 00:00:17.730
But really, I'm a nerd
who likes to code,

00:00:17.730 --> 00:00:20.700
and Jessica is a nerd who
likes science and coding.

00:00:20.700 --> 00:00:25.290
Today, we're going to be talking
about our package, our R package,

00:00:25.290 --> 00:00:26.010
Tplyr.

00:00:26.010 --> 00:00:29.130
And where I want
to start with this

00:00:29.130 --> 00:00:32.729
is a little bit about why we
made it, because typically I'm

00:00:32.729 --> 00:00:34.530
speaking at industry
conferences that

00:00:34.530 --> 00:00:36.660
are pharma-centric
and specifically

00:00:36.660 --> 00:00:39.210
our pocket of pharma
that we work in,

00:00:39.210 --> 00:00:41.640
which is late-phase
clinical trials.

00:00:41.640 --> 00:00:45.060
So the first thing to
understand about programming

00:00:45.060 --> 00:00:49.200
for late phase is that we're not
doing as much exploratory work,

00:00:49.200 --> 00:00:51.510
which is what a lot of
the tools in the R ecosystem

00:00:51.510 --> 00:00:55.470
is built for allowing you
to explore your data, model

00:00:55.470 --> 00:00:57.430
and do all sorts of things.

00:00:57.430 --> 00:00:59.940
But when you're in late phase,
a lot of what you need to do

00:00:59.940 --> 00:01:01.360
is already laid out for you.

00:01:01.360 --> 00:01:03.030
It's highly standardized.

00:01:03.030 --> 00:01:05.670
You already have basically
all of the outputs

00:01:05.670 --> 00:01:06.790
that you need to create.

00:01:06.790 --> 00:01:10.230
So you're really trying to
operate more like a factory.

00:01:10.230 --> 00:01:12.750
So the first kind of
principle of why we made

00:01:12.750 --> 00:01:15.690
Tplyr, is that summary tables
are highly repetitive,

00:01:15.690 --> 00:01:17.640
so your code should
be highly reusable.

00:01:17.640 --> 00:01:19.440
You want to operate
like a factory,

00:01:19.440 --> 00:01:23.557
you're putting out maybe 300
tables for a single trial.

00:01:23.557 --> 00:01:25.140
So you need to get
through that volume

00:01:25.140 --> 00:01:26.765
and you need to get
through it quickly.

00:01:26.765 --> 00:01:28.950
So you really want to be
able to reuse that code.

00:01:28.950 --> 00:01:33.420
The second principle is that
actually for the outputs

00:01:33.420 --> 00:01:35.550
that we create,
summarizing the data

00:01:35.550 --> 00:01:37.030
isn't what takes a lot of time.

00:01:37.030 --> 00:01:39.990
It's easy enough to pop a
variable into count

00:01:39.990 --> 00:01:42.743
or summarize and do some
means and standard deviations

00:01:42.743 --> 00:01:43.660
and get those numbers.

00:01:43.660 --> 00:01:44.830
That doesn't take much time.

00:01:44.830 --> 00:01:46.290
The cosmetics of
getting it to look

00:01:46.290 --> 00:01:48.600
the way that your
statistician acts asks you to

00:01:48.600 --> 00:01:50.407
is what takes a lot of time.

00:01:50.407 --> 00:01:52.740
So the other thing that we
tried to build in the package

00:01:52.740 --> 00:01:54.998
is that text forwarding of
numbers or text formatting

00:01:54.998 --> 00:01:56.290
of numbers can be very tedious.

00:01:56.290 --> 00:01:59.070
So we wanted to make
it intuitive and easy,

00:01:59.070 --> 00:02:02.370
but this package has been out
for two years at this point,

00:02:02.370 --> 00:02:04.020
and I've talked
about this enough

00:02:04.020 --> 00:02:06.630
that my team is definitely
sick about hearing it.

00:02:06.630 --> 00:02:10.650
So the new thing that is the
last principle of what we've

00:02:10.650 --> 00:02:14.040
finally gotten there
with is that we

00:02:14.040 --> 00:02:16.560
want to make
Tplyr more friendly

00:02:16.560 --> 00:02:17.950
for interactive tables.

00:02:17.950 --> 00:02:22.313
So back in 2020, we work
with a lot of different SAS

00:02:22.313 --> 00:02:23.730
programming groups
who are looking

00:02:23.730 --> 00:02:27.450
to adopt R, and the question
that I get constantly

00:02:27.450 --> 00:02:30.720
is, why R? And the point
that was made to me early

00:02:30.720 --> 00:02:33.540
on that resonated that
I've never really forgotten

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:36.210
is that someone told me
that the cost of doing

00:02:36.210 --> 00:02:37.680
an additional,
say, adverse event

00:02:37.680 --> 00:02:39.900
table for a trial
within their company

00:02:39.900 --> 00:02:43.290
is free because you just
pop it into a SAS macro

00:02:43.290 --> 00:02:45.430
and out comes the
table and you're done.

00:02:45.430 --> 00:02:48.210
And when you're trying to
talk about cost savings

00:02:48.210 --> 00:02:49.480
and what R can do for you

00:02:49.480 --> 00:02:51.840
in that area,
that's a hard thing

00:02:51.840 --> 00:02:54.420
to compete against when
the output is free.

00:02:54.420 --> 00:02:57.370
So what does R
really have to offer?

00:02:57.370 --> 00:02:59.760
And that gets into the
world of R Markdown and Shiny

00:02:59.760 --> 00:03:02.220
and interactivity, and
that's where we really want

00:03:02.220 --> 00:03:04.710
to make things more accessible.

00:03:04.710 --> 00:03:07.740
So just to give a little bit
of background on the package

00:03:07.740 --> 00:03:08.760
itself.

00:03:08.760 --> 00:03:12.390
Tplyr, it's an R package
that we released back in 2020

00:03:12.390 --> 00:03:16.920
and I shamelessly took the name
from dplyr and the principle

00:03:16.920 --> 00:03:21.060
from dplyr that it's
a package, a grammar

00:03:21.060 --> 00:03:23.070
of clinical summary tables.

00:03:23.070 --> 00:03:27.270
So the reason that we try to
make it a grammar of summary

00:03:27.270 --> 00:03:30.300
tables is that we wanted
to take your focus off

00:03:30.300 --> 00:03:33.240
of doing the actual summary and
thinking about the algorithms

00:03:33.240 --> 00:03:35.310
that you need to write
to do the summarization

00:03:35.310 --> 00:03:37.500
and how you get the
numbers that you want,

00:03:37.500 --> 00:03:41.880
and really design it more based
off of describing the output

00:03:41.880 --> 00:03:43.240
that you want to create.

00:03:43.240 --> 00:03:45.060
So shifting that
focus of just saying,

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:48.210
this is what I want to
build. A statistician

00:03:48.210 --> 00:03:50.850
typically what you
get your CSR and you

00:03:50.850 --> 00:03:54.408
get the mocks of the tables
that you need to produce

00:03:54.408 --> 00:03:56.700
and that's going to have
different characteristics that

00:03:56.700 --> 00:03:58.117
are going to need
to be fulfilled.

00:03:58.117 --> 00:04:02.310
So we wanted Tplyr's syntax to
focus on describing the output

00:04:02.310 --> 00:04:03.910
and building up the
characteristics.

00:04:03.910 --> 00:04:06.030
And then saying, go
and build this for me.

00:04:06.030 --> 00:04:09.760
The last thing was that we
also wanted to really draw

00:04:09.760 --> 00:04:10.770
our line in the sand.

00:04:10.770 --> 00:04:15.000
I did not want to anchor into
any specific reporting package

00:04:15.000 --> 00:04:15.880
itself.

00:04:15.880 --> 00:04:19.600
I favored huxtable early on
because it had good RTF support.

00:04:19.600 --> 00:04:22.530
Gt has been building up
over the last few years

00:04:22.530 --> 00:04:24.020
and it's had an amazing place.

00:04:24.020 --> 00:04:27.417
Jessica loves flextable,
and then in the Shiny world

00:04:27.417 --> 00:04:28.750
you have things like reactable.

00:04:28.750 --> 00:04:32.040
So I wanted to make the things
that come out of our package

00:04:32.040 --> 00:04:36.750
really easily accessible
to any styling package

00:04:36.750 --> 00:04:37.750
that you want to create.

00:04:37.750 --> 00:04:39.125
And we just wanted
to really stop

00:04:39.125 --> 00:04:40.917
at the character
formatting and the numbers.

00:04:40.917 --> 00:04:42.630
So that annoying
part was done for you

00:04:42.630 --> 00:04:44.797
and that you can pop it
into some other library that

00:04:44.797 --> 00:04:46.100
will actually make it pretty.

00:04:46.100 --> 00:04:47.308
I've never been good at that.

00:04:47.308 --> 00:04:49.960
That's why I hired Maya.

00:04:49.960 --> 00:04:55.180
So to describe Tplyr itself, I want
to thank Christina Fillmore

00:04:55.180 --> 00:04:58.240
for giving me the best metaphor
for this that I've had,

00:04:58.240 --> 00:05:00.220
which is think about cake.

00:05:00.220 --> 00:05:03.732
So cake can take a lot of forms,
it can have a lot of flavors

00:05:03.732 --> 00:05:05.690
and it can have a lot of
different decorations.

00:05:05.690 --> 00:05:07.570
You can have multiple
layers into your cake,

00:05:07.570 --> 00:05:09.760
and those things can look
differently depending

00:05:09.760 --> 00:05:11.000
on how you build it up.

00:05:11.000 --> 00:05:14.260
And that's really what a
Tplyr table ends up being.

00:05:14.260 --> 00:05:16.690
So for a demographics
table, which

00:05:16.690 --> 00:05:19.420
I have a concise
example of up here,

00:05:19.420 --> 00:05:21.730
we have three different
variables from our data set

00:05:21.730 --> 00:05:22.880
that are being summarized.

00:05:22.880 --> 00:05:24.630
So we had the age group
of subjects, which

00:05:24.630 --> 00:05:26.000
is a categorical variable.

00:05:26.000 --> 00:05:28.750
We have age, which is
a continuous variable,

00:05:28.750 --> 00:05:31.720
and then we have race, which is
another categorical variable.

00:05:31.720 --> 00:05:33.400
So for the
categorical variables,

00:05:33.400 --> 00:05:35.830
you're going to count, you're
going to represent percents,

00:05:35.830 --> 00:05:37.690
and then for the
continuous variables,

00:05:37.690 --> 00:05:40.023
you're going to do different
distribution or descriptive

00:05:40.023 --> 00:05:42.310
statistics for your n,
mean, standard deviation, so

00:05:42.310 --> 00:05:43.340
on and so forth.

00:05:43.340 --> 00:05:46.210
And then those things are all
going to be combined together

00:05:46.210 --> 00:05:48.585
and they're going to have
their different characteristics

00:05:48.585 --> 00:05:49.960
of how you want that styled.

00:05:49.960 --> 00:05:52.900
And we're going to bake
our cake and pop that out

00:05:52.900 --> 00:05:55.450
and you get the dataframe
all collected together

00:05:55.450 --> 00:05:57.670
and assembled and
ready formatted

00:05:57.670 --> 00:06:01.920
so that you can go and put that
into some sort of presentation.

00:06:01.920 --> 00:06:03.910
But our focus here today

00:06:03.910 --> 00:06:07.170
and what we're talking
about for Tplyr today

00:06:07.170 --> 00:06:09.880
is what metadata can
really bring to the table.

00:06:09.880 --> 00:06:11.820
So the question that
we're trying to answer

00:06:11.820 --> 00:06:13.920
and what we're trying
to make accessible here

00:06:13.920 --> 00:06:16.980
is that when you look at
a result on the table,

00:06:16.980 --> 00:06:19.343
asking the question, what
data produced that result?

00:06:19.343 --> 00:06:20.760
So if I look at
this table, I know

00:06:20.760 --> 00:06:24.000
that I have six subjects in
the low dose subgroup who

00:06:24.000 --> 00:06:27.100
are Black or African American.
But who are the six subjects?

00:06:27.100 --> 00:06:28.740
What are the data
that some or were

00:06:28.740 --> 00:06:32.380
summarized to actually
produce that result?

00:06:32.380 --> 00:06:35.590
If I'm looking at the median
of the high dose group,

00:06:35.590 --> 00:06:37.550
again, what data
produced that result?

00:06:37.550 --> 00:06:38.750
What subjects were fed in?

00:06:38.750 --> 00:06:41.650
What was the data that went
into my dplyr summarize

00:06:41.650 --> 00:06:43.420
and went through
the median function

00:06:43.420 --> 00:06:45.200
to actually produce that result?

00:06:45.200 --> 00:06:46.960
Because these are
questions that are often

00:06:46.960 --> 00:06:49.120
asked when you want to
drill down into your data

00:06:49.120 --> 00:06:50.910
and understand more about it.

00:06:50.910 --> 00:06:55.030
So specifically, when we talk
about metadata within Tplyr,

00:06:55.030 --> 00:06:57.130
we're asking two
different questions.

00:06:57.130 --> 00:06:59.380
What variables were
necessary to derive

00:06:59.380 --> 00:07:02.710
that result? And what filters
were necessary to derive

00:07:02.710 --> 00:07:03.520
that result?

00:07:03.520 --> 00:07:05.117
So really, what is
the slice of data

00:07:05.117 --> 00:07:07.450
picking out the different
columns and the different rows

00:07:07.450 --> 00:07:11.380
that were actually necessary
to produce the result that you

00:07:11.380 --> 00:07:12.880
see on the table?

00:07:12.880 --> 00:07:15.880
And here I will hand
it over to Jess.

00:07:15.880 --> 00:07:20.800
OK so thanks, Mike, for
giving us a great intro

00:07:20.800 --> 00:07:24.440
on why you created Tplyr.

00:07:24.440 --> 00:07:29.170
And now we've talked about
what do we mean by metadata?

00:07:29.170 --> 00:07:33.690
So how are we going to
harness this metadata?

00:07:33.690 --> 00:07:36.300
So in this process
you've created

00:07:36.300 --> 00:07:40.140
your Tplyr table, which
produces your summary

00:07:40.140 --> 00:07:40.870
dataframe.

00:07:40.870 --> 00:07:42.220
These are the results you see.

00:07:42.220 --> 00:07:45.810
You would see them on paper
if you printed it out.

00:07:45.810 --> 00:07:47.860
And additionally,
what we're doing here,

00:07:47.860 --> 00:07:53.520
now, is that including this
table metadata, that metadata,

00:07:53.520 --> 00:07:55.970
remember, those are the
names and the variables used

00:07:55.970 --> 00:07:59.760
to create that result. And
we can use that metadata

00:07:59.760 --> 00:08:02.130
to create the
relevant subset and we

00:08:02.130 --> 00:08:06.150
can wrap all of this in a
really easy Shiny application

00:08:06.150 --> 00:08:09.810
to create an interactive table
that, upon a click action, will

00:08:09.810 --> 00:08:14.610
give us both our summary
table and the actual data

00:08:14.610 --> 00:08:19.890
that produced that result.
So what does this look like?

00:08:19.890 --> 00:08:20.810
And building it.

00:08:20.810 --> 00:08:22.400
So how do you get that metadata?

00:08:22.400 --> 00:08:24.310
How do you do you have to save?

00:08:24.310 --> 00:08:25.450
It's very simple.

00:08:25.450 --> 00:08:28.330
So creating the metadata,
all you have to do

00:08:28.330 --> 00:08:29.450
is change the argument.

00:08:29.450 --> 00:08:30.950
Metadata equals true.

00:08:30.950 --> 00:08:36.250
And what will happen is
you're the metadata for this

00:08:36.250 --> 00:08:38.780
for your table, we'll just
be created automatically.

00:08:41.740 --> 00:08:45.070
For example, we have our
result output data frame

00:08:45.070 --> 00:08:47.290
and now you can
see what's actually

00:08:47.290 --> 00:08:50.900
added to the table is the
Tplyr metadata object.

00:08:50.900 --> 00:08:54.940
So each cell contains this
object and this object

00:08:54.940 --> 00:08:56.030
is the metadata.

00:08:56.030 --> 00:08:58.060
Again, those variables,
those names that

00:08:58.060 --> 00:09:01.920
created that
specific result cell.

00:09:01.920 --> 00:09:04.400
As we were talking
about earlier,

00:09:04.400 --> 00:09:07.790
this is indicated by a
row ID and a column ID.

00:09:07.790 --> 00:09:11.920
And we can find go from there
to find who are these people?

00:09:11.920 --> 00:09:14.100
They're not just numbers
in the summary table.

00:09:14.100 --> 00:09:16.980
These are subjects
in a clinical trial.

00:09:16.980 --> 00:09:18.340
So we've got this metadata.

00:09:18.340 --> 00:09:19.330
How do we extract it?

00:09:19.330 --> 00:09:21.420
What do we see?

00:09:21.420 --> 00:09:22.380
Can we get to this?

00:09:22.380 --> 00:09:24.730
As I mentioned, the
input is really simple.

00:09:24.730 --> 00:09:28.420
You supply at a Tplyr table,
a row ID, and a column name.

00:09:28.420 --> 00:09:32.850
And the output I really like
about this is that you can see,

00:09:32.850 --> 00:09:35.860
OK, these are the names that are
required to create this result.

00:09:35.860 --> 00:09:38.460
I already know what
columns I'm looking at.

00:09:38.460 --> 00:09:40.230
These are the
filters and you can

00:09:40.230 --> 00:09:43.480
see exactly what it looks like.

00:09:43.480 --> 00:09:47.900
What are those, the filters
that are going into that result?

00:09:47.900 --> 00:09:49.980
Saying, great, I see this.

00:09:49.980 --> 00:09:51.630
Where the heck is that subset?

00:09:51.630 --> 00:09:53.840
What is this data?

00:09:53.840 --> 00:09:57.650
This is a really simple
function too. Again, give it.

00:09:57.650 --> 00:09:59.700
It's a new function,
get meta subset.

00:09:59.700 --> 00:10:03.980
You provide the
same inputs and it

00:10:03.980 --> 00:10:06.620
produces that subset,
that subset of data

00:10:06.620 --> 00:10:08.220
that produced your results.

00:10:08.220 --> 00:10:13.580
You can see exactly
who is in that result.

00:10:13.580 --> 00:10:18.960
Additionally, this get meta
subset function includes

00:10:18.960 --> 00:10:22.550
an additional argument to
add relevant columns that

00:10:22.550 --> 00:10:26.330
might give your subset a
little additional context.

00:10:26.330 --> 00:10:33.000
It defaults to use USUBJID, which
is for those you in the clinical world.

00:10:33.000 --> 00:10:39.430
It's a C specific variable
that these data sets have.

00:10:39.430 --> 00:10:42.350
However, you can change
it to whatever you want.

00:10:42.350 --> 00:10:47.990
This is the only place where any
clinical or C specific defaults

00:10:47.990 --> 00:10:50.560
or language is used
in this package.

00:10:50.560 --> 00:10:54.590
We really want Tplyr to be
extensible beyond just using

00:10:54.590 --> 00:11:01.320
finished C-disc data, but this is
the one place where you see it.

00:11:01.320 --> 00:11:04.590
So to summarize, we've created
our Tplyr table, which

00:11:04.590 --> 00:11:06.930
includes our summary results.

00:11:06.930 --> 00:11:10.060
We've got our relevant
metadata, our filters and names.

00:11:10.060 --> 00:11:12.110
We can create the
subset to see that.

00:11:12.110 --> 00:11:15.610
What does this actually look
like in an interactive table?

00:11:15.610 --> 00:11:18.670
So let me give you
a quick little demo.

00:11:18.670 --> 00:11:21.060
What you're going to see is

00:11:21.060 --> 00:11:24.690
the most barebones, basic,
Shiny app ever created.

00:11:24.690 --> 00:11:27.480
I'm actually I'm pretty
proud of this fact.

00:11:27.480 --> 00:11:29.980
I didn't create it, but I could.

00:11:29.980 --> 00:11:34.240
And I'm not a Shiny developer.

00:11:34.240 --> 00:11:34.880
It's gone.

00:11:34.880 --> 00:11:36.070
Here it is.

00:11:36.070 --> 00:11:38.140
All its glory.

00:11:38.140 --> 00:11:39.140
In fact, it's so simple.

00:11:39.140 --> 00:11:42.850
It's 85 lines long, which I
had to look up and was again,

00:11:42.850 --> 00:11:43.790
you can see the code.

00:11:43.790 --> 00:11:45.580
We will provide that.

00:11:45.580 --> 00:11:47.005
So what do we have here?

00:11:47.005 --> 00:11:47.880
You can drive around.

00:11:50.470 --> 00:11:52.360
So you want to see, as
we mentioned earlier,

00:11:52.360 --> 00:11:57.190
we were looking at who are those
eight subjects in the placebo

00:11:57.190 --> 00:11:59.030
group that are Black
or African-American.

00:11:59.030 --> 00:12:04.230
You click on the result and
your metadata subset pops up.

00:12:04.230 --> 00:12:06.490
You can see it, you can check.

00:12:06.490 --> 00:12:09.860
So this is incredibly useful.

00:12:09.860 --> 00:12:13.930
I as someone who spent a lot of
time looking at summary tables,

00:12:13.930 --> 00:12:15.940
then looking at a
really long listing,

00:12:15.940 --> 00:12:19.270
trying to find those
people individually

00:12:19.270 --> 00:12:22.240
or programming it on the
side while you're comparing

00:12:22.240 --> 00:12:26.050
at a summary table. To be able
to see all of these things

00:12:26.050 --> 00:12:30.040
at once, to take your
review from a sort of very

00:12:30.040 --> 00:12:33.640
manual process to something
that's interactive,

00:12:33.640 --> 00:12:35.830
that's quick, that's fast.

00:12:35.830 --> 00:12:37.690
It's just all about
what we're about,

00:12:37.690 --> 00:12:39.880
trying to make things
quicker, faster and easier,

00:12:39.880 --> 00:12:43.420
and to bring pharma
along in that process.

00:12:43.420 --> 00:12:47.020
Slowly.

00:12:47.020 --> 00:12:48.280
OK that's great.

00:12:48.280 --> 00:12:50.400
Regular summary table.

00:12:50.400 --> 00:12:51.940
These are pretty common.

00:12:51.940 --> 00:12:56.182
What about if you want to?

00:12:56.182 --> 00:12:56.890
When you want to.

00:12:56.890 --> 00:13:00.360
If you were in an example of
where you have a summary table

00:13:00.360 --> 00:13:02.730
and you've included some
additional statistical analyses

00:13:02.730 --> 00:13:05.910
or models, and you have those
results that you want to share,

00:13:05.910 --> 00:13:09.010
and you also want to include
the metadata for that test.

00:13:09.010 --> 00:13:10.650
You want to know
exactly what subjects

00:13:10.650 --> 00:13:12.540
went into that analysis.

00:13:12.540 --> 00:13:17.800
Tplyr can extend
and append the metadata.

00:13:17.800 --> 00:13:19.860
You can create a
new metadata object,

00:13:19.860 --> 00:13:21.600
you can add those
filters and names that

00:13:21.600 --> 00:13:25.210
will help created the results.

00:13:25.210 --> 00:13:27.390
And in addition, and
my favorite part is

00:13:27.390 --> 00:13:30.730
you don't actually need a
Tplyr table to do this.

00:13:30.730 --> 00:13:33.030
This will work
with a data frame.

00:13:33.030 --> 00:13:35.650
I was just recently thinking
about the context of this.

00:13:35.650 --> 00:13:39.623
In my way former life, I was
an evolutionary biologist.

00:13:39.623 --> 00:13:41.040
I worked with a
lot of butterflies

00:13:41.040 --> 00:13:42.960
and used to  put them
in models and I was

00:13:42.960 --> 00:13:45.210
like, man, if I could
have seen the subset that

00:13:45.210 --> 00:13:47.668
would go into that at that
time, it would be really useful.

00:13:47.668 --> 00:13:49.800
So I can see how I personally
would have taken this

00:13:49.800 --> 00:13:52.210
and, and used it in my own work.

00:13:52.210 --> 00:13:55.290
So again, extending
that metadata,

00:13:55.290 --> 00:13:58.510
adding that metadata to you
directly to your results.

00:13:58.510 --> 00:14:00.730
So you can pull
that up as needed.

00:14:00.730 --> 00:14:03.680
Also quick this Shiny
up again, really simple.

00:14:03.680 --> 00:14:07.810
So now we're looking at, we have
the results of a dose-response

00:14:07.810 --> 00:14:11.450
model between

00:14:11.450 --> 00:14:12.600
high and low, I think.

00:14:12.600 --> 00:14:14.330
I can't see that well.

00:14:14.330 --> 00:14:16.665
But again, you see who is
in who's in this model?

00:14:16.665 --> 00:14:17.790
What are the subjects here?

00:14:17.790 --> 00:14:20.480
What is the data that's
included in this model?

00:14:20.480 --> 00:14:23.310
You can click on this really
simple interactive table.

00:14:23.310 --> 00:14:26.430
And the results are produced.

00:14:26.430 --> 00:14:29.120
And right there for you, it's
a really quick and snazzy way

00:14:29.120 --> 00:14:32.420
to see your results and the
metadata that created them

00:14:32.420 --> 00:14:34.430
all at once.

00:14:34.430 --> 00:14:37.010
And with that, like
I said, we're really

00:14:37.010 --> 00:14:39.700
excited at how we're sort of

00:14:39.700 --> 00:14:43.270
getting some of these, the
idea of taking these formerly

00:14:43.270 --> 00:14:46.420
paper clinical tables
and moving them

00:14:46.420 --> 00:14:48.670
into more of a screen
friendly format

00:14:48.670 --> 00:14:50.980
into a quick review
format in a way

00:14:50.980 --> 00:14:52.880
that you can keep
moving forward.

00:14:52.880 --> 00:14:54.660
Thank you.

